RIFE (Real-Time Intermediate Flow Estimation) — vendored network code
=======================================================================

Upstream project: Practical-RIFE
Upstream URL:     https://github.com/hzwer/Practical-RIFE
Upstream licence: MIT (copyright (c) 2021 hzwer)

Model version:    RIFE 4.25, released 2024-09-19 (per the upstream README's
                   "Trained Model" table, which recommends 4.25 as the
                   default for most scenes).

Vendored on:      2026-08-14

What was vendored
------------------
`ifnet.py` in this directory contains the `IFNet` network architecture
(`Head`, `ResConv`, `IFBlock`, `IFNet`, and the `warp` helper) that
upstream ships as `train_log/IFNet_HDv3.py` and `model/warplayer.py`.
Only the network classes were kept — the `Model` training/inference
wrapper (`train_log/RIFE_HDv3.py`), the optimizer, loss functions, and
dataset code were not reproduced.

The `warp` grid-sampling helper is inlined into `ifnet.py` (upstream keeps
it in a separate `model/warplayer.py` module that is not part of this
vendored subset) and has one behavioural fix: upstream builds its sampling
grid on a module-level `device` that is fixed to CUDA-if-available at
import time, which breaks CPU inference on any machine that also has a
CUDA device. The vendored version builds the grid on the input tensor's
own device instead. This is the only intentional deviation from upstream;
everything else is unmodified apart from the import adjustment needed to
remove the dependency on the separate `model.warplayer` module.

Provenance of the vendored text
--------------------------------
Practical-RIFE distributes each model version as a Google Drive archive
(architecture source + `flownet.pkl` weights bundled together) rather than
committing `train_log/` to git, so the exact file cannot be fetched from
the upstream git history. The architecture text used here was taken from:

- https://github.com/HThanh-how/RIFEv4.25_0919/blob/main/train_log/IFNet_HDv3.py
  — a community re-upload of the unzipped 4.25 (2024-09-19) archive,
  matching the release date and filenames the upstream README describes.

Its channel dimensions (5 IFBlocks, c=[192, 128, 96, 64, 32], Head with a
4-channel output) were cross-checked against an independent, actively
maintained MIT-licensed port of the same version:

- https://github.com/HolyWu/vs-rife/blob/main/vsrife/IFNet_HDv3_v4_25.py
  (HolyWu/vs-rife, MIT licence, copyright (c) 2021 HolyWu)

The two agree on architecture shape; vs-rife's version was not used
directly because its `forward()` has been refactored to take precomputed
head features and warp grids as extra arguments (an optimisation for its
own streaming pipeline), which would no longer match the upstream
"unmodified apart from import fixes" form this project vendors.

Weights
-------
`flownet.pkl` (RIFE 4.25) is downloaded from a direct HTTPS URL, not
committed to this repository (`models/` is gitignored):

    https://github.com/HolyWu/vs-rife/releases/download/model/flownet_v4.25.pkl

This is the same v4.25 weight file the vs-rife project's own auto-download
feature fetches; it is hosted as a GitHub release asset, so — unlike the
upstream Google Drive links — it is fetchable with a plain HTTP GET.

